 above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435357 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435357 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435357 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435357 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$, and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{4}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435357 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e2ef874-d6ff-53a7-81b2-d096ed751911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435357 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{4}}={{(\\frac{3}{4})}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435358 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435358 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435358 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435358 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435358 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa6c549b-f344-52f9-9015-63f22befbecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435358 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a6387a3-d6c3-514a-adf3-03c92255b693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186435358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435360 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186435361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435360 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56aac62a-1ac3-595b-9746-f6129816ec3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435360 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435361 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/r